1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,820
This is Source running locally. Let’s create a task. I enter the goal, add a

2
00:00:05,820 --> 00:00:10,340
requirement, and say how we’ll check the result. Source saves it as a Work item.

3
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:15,820
Now I claim it and start a session. Watch the status change from open, to claimed,

4
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:20,060
to in progress. The session gives the work a record of who started it and where

5
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:25,140
it began. This task is a separate UI example; next we’ll run the three-client

6
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:25,940
rehearsal.

7
00:00:27,875 --> 00:00:32,795
Here is the rehearsal running against real Git repositories and Source’s API.

8
00:00:32,795 --> 00:00:37,155
The terminal shows output captured from that run, with pauses added so we can

9
00:00:37,155 --> 00:00:41,955
read it. Three scripted clients start from the same commit. One changes the

10
00:00:41,955 --> 00:00:47,635
theme. One updates the documentation. One changes the page size. They claim

11
00:00:47,635 --> 00:00:52,915
separate tasks, start sessions, and submit proposals. Their execution intervals

12
00:00:52,915 --> 00:00:59,235
overlap. Each client also tries to approve its own change. Source returns HTTP

13
00:00:59,235 --> 00:01:04,635
403 for all three. These credentials can contribute code, but they cannot approve

14
00:01:04,635 --> 00:01:04,995
it.

15
00:01:06,917 --> 00:01:11,437
Back in the browser, I open a change from the rehearsal. The page shows the

16
00:01:11,437 --> 00:01:15,677
request and the two commits being compared. Below that are the checks attached

17
00:01:15,677 --> 00:01:20,517
to this proposal. The rehearsal ran the settings test and a Git diff check.

18
00:01:20,517 --> 00:01:25,477
I evaluate the policy again, then scroll to the evidence. Both records are valid.

19
00:01:25,477 --> 00:01:30,477
The decision has its own record and names the approver. In this demo, a script

20
00:01:30,477 --> 00:01:35,237
plays the maintainer. The checks really ran; the human review is simulated.

21
00:01:37,167 --> 00:01:42,367
Once the theme change lands, the other proposals have an old base. Source blocks

22
00:01:42,367 --> 00:01:46,607
the stale update without moving the shared branch. The documentation client

23
00:01:46,607 --> 00:01:52,367
rebases and submits a new proposal. The page-size client hits a real conflict:

24
00:01:52,367 --> 00:01:57,287
both clients edited the same line in settings dot JSON. The recovery step keeps

25
00:01:57,287 --> 00:02:01,807
the dark theme and sets the page size to twenty. It then reruns the checks and

26
00:02:01,807 --> 00:02:06,407
submits the revised change. Only after the scripted maintainer accepts that

27
00:02:06,407 --> 00:02:09,007
revision does Source advance the shared branch.

28
00:02:10,958 --> 00:02:15,998
Now let’s inspect what landed. I open the repository, switch to History, and

29
00:02:15,998 --> 00:02:20,798
select the page-size commit. The diff shows the change from ten to twenty while

30
00:02:20,798 --> 00:02:26,078
keeping the dark theme. Back in Contents, I open the actual settings file. Both

31
00:02:26,078 --> 00:02:30,478
changes are there. The earlier proposals, their evidence, and their decisions

32
00:02:30,478 --> 00:02:35,598
remain in Source. This is the useful part: we can inspect the final code and

33
00:02:35,598 --> 00:02:37,278
follow the work that produced it.

34
00:02:39,208 --> 00:02:43,768
Now we can run the same collaboration flow against Cloudflare. Source still

35
00:02:43,768 --> 00:02:48,688
runs on this computer. The deployed Worker controls access to an actual Artifacts

36
00:02:48,688 --> 00:02:52,528
repository, which holds the shared Git branch. These lines are captured from

37
00:02:52,528 --> 00:02:57,208
the live run. The Worker creates the repository. Source submits the starting

38
00:02:57,208 --> 00:03:01,888
files as a proposal, checks them, records the maintainer decision, and promotes

39
00:03:01,888 --> 00:03:07,208
the accepted commit. Trying to promote before acceptance fails. The local canonical

40
00:03:07,208 --> 00:03:11,448
guard also rejects deletion of the shared branch. Contributors receive Source

41
00:03:11,448 --> 00:03:16,328
credentials. They do not receive the Worker secret or the Artifacts write token.

42
00:03:18,250 --> 00:03:22,970
Three separate client processes now start from that shared commit. Their work

43
00:03:22,970 --> 00:03:29,330
overlaps, and all three attempts at self-approval return HTTP 403. Source checks

44
00:03:29,330 --> 00:03:34,010
the theme proposal and the scripted maintainer accepts it. Then we test a failure

45
00:03:34,010 --> 00:03:38,770
that is easy to miss: the cloud accepts the push, but Source does not receive

46
00:03:38,770 --> 00:03:43,290
a success result. The harness deliberately throws an error after the real Git

47
00:03:43,290 --> 00:03:47,850
push succeeds. Source leaves the Work item accepted, rather than calling it

48
00:03:47,850 --> 00:03:52,730
finished. On retry, it reads the remote commit, confirms that the change already

49
00:03:52,730 --> 00:03:57,690
landed, and closes the work. The trace confirms there was just one push.

50
00:03:59,625 --> 00:04:04,105
Here is the Source record from that live run. I open the accepted change and

51
00:04:04,105 --> 00:04:08,585
inspect its evidence and decision. The checks ran against the proposed files.

52
00:04:08,585 --> 00:04:13,465
The decision identifies the scripted maintainer. The repository history shows

53
00:04:13,465 --> 00:04:18,225
the commits that Source confirmed in Artifacts. This is a hybrid deployment:

54
00:04:18,225 --> 00:04:22,345
the review application and its data are local, while the Worker and canonical

55
00:04:22,345 --> 00:04:27,225
Git repository are on Cloudflare. The agents are scripted clients, and approval

56
00:04:27,225 --> 00:04:31,705
is scripted too. We are testing the workflow and its boundaries, not claiming

57
00:04:31,705 --> 00:04:36,025
that a language model performed the work or that a person reviewed each patch.

58
00:04:37,958 --> 00:04:43,158
The remaining proposals now have an old base. The live bridge rejects them without

59
00:04:43,158 --> 00:04:48,238
overwriting the remote branch. The documentation client rebases cleanly. The

60
00:04:48,238 --> 00:04:53,518
page-size client reaches the same real conflict we saw earlier. After resolution,

61
00:04:53,518 --> 00:04:57,878
it submits a revised change, reruns the checks, and receives a new decision.

62
00:04:57,878 --> 00:05:03,078
Source promotes the accepted revision to Artifacts. Finally, a separate Git

63
00:05:03,078 --> 00:05:07,318
clone downloads the cloud repository. Its commit matches Source's canonical

64
00:05:07,318 --> 00:05:12,838
cache. The files contain the dark theme, page size twenty, and the review documentation.

65
00:05:12,838 --> 00:05:17,998
All three client Work items are closed. The report preserves the remote address,

66
00:05:17,998 --> 00:05:20,518
commit identifiers, and recovery checks.

67
00:05:22,458 --> 00:05:28,018
Source is public and MIT licensed. The repository includes the local rehearsals,

68
00:05:28,018 --> 00:05:32,818
the live integration command, and the verification report. This run proves the

69
00:05:32,818 --> 00:05:37,018
review and promotion flow through real Workers and Artifacts, including stale

70
00:05:37,018 --> 00:05:41,898
proposals and recovery after a lost acknowledgement. It does not establish production

71
00:05:41,898 --> 00:05:46,818
capacity or replace a security review. The result is a working prototype you

72
00:05:46,818 --> 00:05:51,658
can inspect: from a task, through a proposal and evidence, to an accepted commit

73
00:05:51,658 --> 00:05:52,538
in the cloud.
